{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-48583",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b-3(0)}+2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{b}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(x+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{b}+2 & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =3-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{b} & =1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
